# The Tale of the Two Villages

Long ago, two villages stood on opposite sides of a great river. The upstream village of Giva received abundant rainfall, while downstream Recipra often suffered drought.

One season, Giva's elder proposed sending half their water downstream. The villagers feared scarcity, but the elder said, "Trust creates abundance." As the water flowed down, the river's magic tripled it, flooding Recipra with life-giving resources.

The people of Recipra rejoiced. Their council gathered to decide: "Giva risked much. They sent half their water, which became more than enough for us. We should return generously, honoring their trust."

They sent back two-thirds of what they received. When it arrived in Giva, both villages prospered - Giva had more than they started with, and Recipra retained plenty.

This became their way. Each season, Giva would send water in faith, trusting it would multiply. Each season, Recipra would return generously, honoring that trust. The river's magic only worked when both villages played their part: one giving courageously, the other reciprocating fairly.

The villages learned that prosperity flows not from hoarding, but from the sacred cycle of trust and return.
