# The Myth of the Two Wells

In ancient times, two villages stood on opposite sides of a great desert. Each village had a magical well: when water was poured into one well, it would flow to the other, tripled in volume.

The First Village was blessed with rain each season, receiving five full buckets. They faced a choice: keep their water, or pour it into the well, trusting it would return.

At first, both villages were cautious, sending little. They remained thirsty.

Then, a wise elder spoke: "The well multiplies what we give, but only if we trust it to flow back. One village must pour generously into the well, and the other must honor that trust by returning enough for both to flourish."

The First Village poured three buckets into the well. It became nine in the Second Village. Remembering the elder's wisdom, the Second Village returned five buckets, keeping four for themselves.

Now the First Village had seven buckets total—more than their original five! The Second Village had four—more than they'd ever known.

Season after season, they continued this sacred exchange. Both villages prospered, for they understood: **generosity multiplied returns abundance, but only when trust flows both ways.**

The well still stands, waiting for those wise enough to trust.
